<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesannepadda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mesannepadda was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> around 2500 BCE, named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> as the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of Ur. His name appears on contemporary objects, including a seal from the Royal Cemetery of Ur and a bead found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, and he also bore the title king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, claiming wider supremacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>. He is one of the earliest Sumerian kings whose existence is confirmed by contemporary sources.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Mesannepada, whose name means &quot;Youngling chosen by An&quot;, is the first king listed for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>. The list credits him with a reign of 80 years and says that he overthrew Lugal-kitun of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, so that kingship was carried to Ur. Its next entries are his son Meskiagnun, who reigned 36 years, then Elulu and Balulu. A reign of 80 years is thought unlikely, and the length of his son's reign was probably added to his. An inscribed bead found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> names him as son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskalamdug">Meskalamdug</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mesannepada appears on objects from his own time. A lapis lazuli bead naming Mesannepada, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> in the so-called Treasure of Ur, a jar of offerings that marked the foundation of a temple there. It was dedicated to the god Lugalkalam. It is unclear how the bead reached Mari, but it suggests some relation between Ur and Mari. Seals from the Royal Cemetery at Ur name Mesannepada, his predecessors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskalamdug">Meskalamdug</a> and Akalamdug, and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puabi">Puabi</a>, and one seal impression calls him king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, a title taken to show his hegemony.</p><p>The Old Babylonian Tummal Inscription lists Mesannepada and his son Meskiagnun among rulers who maintained the main temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and his son Ur-Nungal. This supports their status as overlords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>. Another son, A'annepada, is known from tablets recording that he built a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninhursag">Ninhursag</a>, at modern Ubaid, and he is not named on the king list. A small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> beneath the later structure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> may date from his time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the 1950s Edmund I. Gordon conjectured that Mesannepada and an early king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> named Mesilim were the same man, since their names are interchanged in some proverbs, but this was not conclusive. More recent scholars generally treat them as distinct and place Mesilim at Kish before Mesannepada. His tomb may lie in the Royal Cemetery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, and it has been suggested that the tomb PG 1232 or PG 1237, known as the Great Death-Pit, is his.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mesannepada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.96,46.1&amp;year=-2500" aria-label="Ur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mesannepadda"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3991 4856h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3991 4856h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.96,46.1&amp;year=-2500">What was here in 2500 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
